inal pain"], "excluded_candidates": ["Acute bacterial infection: Leukocytosis is present but normal differential and lack of infectious foci weakens this candidacy", "Diabetic ketoacidosis: Blood glucose is normal and there are no other metabolic acidosis markers", "Medication-induced leukocytosis (e.g., corticosteroids): No glucocorticoid use documented, but other medications remain in scope"], "alarm_features": ["Leukocytosis >6x upper limit of normal", "Constitutional symptoms (weight loss + fatigue)", "Elevated Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase in chronic myeloproliferative process context"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.35, "posterior": 0.22672064777327935, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Subacute weight loss and fatigue (7 weeks) are more characteristic of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms. This provides moderate support for CML while reducing reactive leukocytosis plausibility.", "Elevated LAP has higher specificity for neoplastic (CML) than reactive leukocytosis. This moderately strengthens the chronic neoplasm hypothesis and weakens reactive etiologies."], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP is typical of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms (like CML) but lower in blast-driven myeloid neoplasms. This result weakly contradicts the 'increased blasts' subtype hypothesis.", "A preserved differential with leukocytosis is atypical for myeloid neoplasms with blasts. This weakly supports the idea of a chronic phase process (e.g., CML) and weakly excludes blast-driven myeloid neoplasms."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Presence of bone marrow fibrosis", "History of previous blood cancer", "Fever pattern or bleeding symptoms"], "requestable_discriminators": ["BCR-ABL1/FISH testing", "Bone marrow biopsy for blast count", "Cytogenetics panel"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.25, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.45, "posterior": 0.6558704453441296, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["A preserved differential with leukocytosis is atypical for myeloid neoplasms with blasts. This weakly supports the idea of a chronic phase process (e.g., CML) and weakly excludes blast-driven myeloid neoplasms.", "Elevated LAP has higher specificity for neoplastic (CML) than reactive leukocytosis. This moderately strengthens the chronic neoplasm hypothesis and weakens reactive etiologies."], "evidence_against": ["Splenomegaly is a key physical finding in CML. Its absence reduces suspicion for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms but doesn't rule it out definitively."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Recent weight loss vs chronic anemia history", "Splenomegaly on physical exam", "Family history of MPNs"], "requestable_discriminators": ["JAK2/V617F mutation testing", "CBC with peripheral smear review", "Abdominal ultrasound for splenomegaly"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.15, "posterior": 0.09716599190283401, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP is nonspecific but more consistent with myeloid rather than lymphoid processes. However, it's not clinically decisive against lymphop