{"case_id": "MB_9", "timestep": 2, "case_summary": "A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue. In general, he has been in good health; however, he recently has experienced weight loss, abdominal pain, and general fatigue. He has a medical history of anxiety, diabetes, a fracture of his foot sustained when he tripped, and a recent cold that caused him to miss work for 1 week. His current medications include metformin, insulin, buspirone, vitamin D, calcium, and sodium docusate. His temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 150/100 mmHg, pulse is 90/min, respirations are 18/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam reveals a calm gentleman. A mild systolic murmur is heard in the left upper sternal region. The rest of the physical exam is within normal limits. Laboratory values are ordered as seen below. Hemoglobin: 12 g/dL Hematocrit: 36% Leukocyte count: 66,500/mm^3 with normal differential Platelet count: 177,000/mm^3 Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase: elevated Serum: Na+: 139 mEq/L Cl-: 100 mEq/L K+: 4.3 mEq/L BUN: 20 mg/dL Glucose: 120 mg/dL Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL Ca2+: 10.9 mEq/L AST: 12 U/L ALT: 10 U/L Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma\nB. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia\nC. Chronic myeloid leukemia\nD. Leukemoid reaction\nE. Multiple myeloma\n", "root": {"label": "Subacute Constitutional Syndrome with Elevated White Blood Cell Count, Mild Systolic Murmur, and Possible Hematologic or Inflammatory Aetiology", "time_course": "subacute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.65, "supporting_facts": ["Recent fatigue, weight loss (7 weeks duration not explicitly stated but considered subacute by default)", "Leukocytosis with 66,500/mm³ absolute elevation", "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase", "Normal differential despite leukocytosis", "Mild systolic murmur in left parasternal region", "Borderline elevated temperature (37.5°C)", "Unexplained abdominal pain"], "excluded_candidates": ["Acute bacterial infection: Leukocytosis is present but normal differential and lack of infectious foci weakens this candidacy", "Diabetic ketoacidosis: Blood glucose is normal and there are no other metabolic acidosis markers", "Medication-induced leukocytosis (e.g., corticosteroids): No glucocorticoid use documented, but other medications remain in scope"], "alarm_features": ["Leukocytosis >6x upper limit of normal", "Constitutional symptoms (weight loss + fatigue)", "Elevated Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase in chronic myeloproliferative process context"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.22672064777327935, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Subacute weight loss and fatigue (7 weeks) are more characteristic of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms. This provides moderate support for CML while reducing reactive leukocytosis plausibility.", "Elevated LAP has higher specificity for neoplastic (CML) than reactive leukocytosis. This moderately strengthens the chronic neoplasm hypothesis and weakens reactive etiologies."], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP is typical of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms (like CML) but lower in blast-driven myeloid neoplasms. This result weakly contradicts the 'increased blasts' subtype hypothesis.", "A preserved differential with leukocytosis is atypical for myeloid neoplasms with blasts. This weakly supports the idea of a chronic phase process (e.g., CML) and weakly excludes blast-driven myeloid neoplasms."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Presence of bone marrow fibrosis", "History of previous blood cancer", "Fever pattern or bleeding symptoms"], "requestable_discriminators": ["BCR-ABL1/FISH 