{"case_id": "MB_6", "timestep": 3, "case_summary": "A 2-day-old boy has an episode of vomiting in the hospital nursery. The vomitus was described as “bright green” without any traces of blood. The patient has urinated several times since he was born but has not passed any stool. He was born at 37 weeks of gestation to a 38-year-old G3P3 woman. The pregnancy was uncomplicated and the patient’s mother refused all prenatal testing. The patient’s 2 older siblings are both healthy. His temperature is 98.6°F (37°C), blood pressure is 67/43 mmHg, pulse is 135/min, and respirations are 34/min. On physical exam, the patient has upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, and a single transverse palmar crease. His abdomen is non-tender, firm, and distended. Bowel sounds are hypoactive. Digital rectal exam evacuates a small amount of stool and flatulence. A nasogastric tube is placed to decompress the stomach. The patient’s abdominal radiograph can be seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Duodenal atresia\nB. Hirschsprung disease\nC. Intestinal malrotation\nD. Meconium ileus\nE. Pyloric stenosis\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Neonatal Vomiting with Feacal Incontinence/Non-passing Stool and Distended Abdomen (Intestinal Obstruction Pattern), with Uncharacterised Abdominal Radiograph Abnormality", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.6, "supporting_facts": ["2-day-old age with acute presentation of bright green vomiting (suggesting bile reflux from distal obstruction)", "No stool passed since birth despite urinary output", "Distended abdomen with hypoactive bowel sounds", "Rectal exam yields small stool/flatulence (inconsistent with complete obstructive pattern)", "Tier-1 unresolved abdominal imaging available (Figure A)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Chronic Gastrointestinal Malabsorption Syndrome: inconsistent temporal pattern (acute presentation)", "Maternal Gestational Diabetes Sequelae: patient's mother's refusal of prenatal testing weakens this as primary mechanism compared to structural obstruction"], "alarm_features": ["Congenital anomaly markers (upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, single transverse palmar crease; consistent with Down syndrome)", "Hirschsprung's disease association with physical features and bowel dysfunction"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Anatomic Gastrointestinal Obstruction in Neonates", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.06178127780157502, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Non-bilious green呕吐 in a term neonate with delayed stool passage is moderately suggestive of anatomic obstruction, though rectal evacuation weakens this pattern.", "Neonatal onset of bowel dysfunction is highly consistent with congenital conditions like Hirschsprung disease (B2.1), while inborn errors (B2.2) are more likely to present with electrolyte/absorption abnormalities."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of meconium plug pattern in radiograph reduces likelihood of meconium ileus but doesn't directly contradict motility disorders like Hirschsprung disease.", "Down syndrome physical features and rectal flatus evacuation on exam provide specific support for Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) relative to inborn errors (B2.2)."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Age at onset of symptoms (day 1-3 life typical for duodenal atresia/malrotation vs. meconium ileus)", "Presence of maternal familial history of congenital anomalies"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Abdominal ultrasound for duodenal obstruction (double bubble sign)", "Contrast enema to assess rectal transitional zone in Hirschsprung disease"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Neonatal Intestinal Motility Disorders", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.6647230320699709, "posterior": 0.5296385383373423, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Classic physical stigmata of Down syndrome increase suspicion for Hirschsprung disease, which presents with delayed stool passage and rectal evacuation. This clinically supports B2 over B1.", "Rectal evacuation of stool and flatus suggests partial intestinal patency, which is more consistent with Hirschsprung disease than a complete anatomical block."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Physical exam signs of congenital syndromes (e.g., Down syndrome features)", "Bowel motility during clinical exam (delayed peristalsis or 'lead pipe' abdomen)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Rectal biopsy for megacolon detection in Hirschsprung disease", "Abdominal X-ray (Figure A) for Hirschsprung's 'tapered rectum' sign"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Bowel Dysfunction Due to Inborn Errors of Metabolism", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.032950014827506674, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Healthy siblings reduce support for inherited metabolic disorders (B2.2/B3). The Down syndrome phenotype is more diagnostically relevant to Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) than metabolic causes."]}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.4653061224489796, "posterior": 0.4732940129823059, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Neonatal onset of bowel dysfunction is highly consistent with congenital conditions like Hirschsprung disease (B2.1), while inborn errors (B2.2) are more likely to present with electrolyte/absorption abnormalities.", "Healthy siblings reduce support for inherited metabolic disorders (B2.2/B3). The Down syndrome phenotype is more diagnostically relevant to Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) than metabolic causes."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of delayed passage of meconium (in this case, no stool passed since birth supports the diagnosis)", "Presence of Down syndrome features (upslanting palpebral fissures, single palmar crease) which are strongly associated with Hirschsprung disease"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells confirming aganglionosis", "Rectal manometry (out-of-phase contractions) to differentiate from other motility disorders"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Neonatal Onset of Inborn Error of Intestinal Epithelial Function", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.05634452535503642, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Healthy siblings reduce support for inherited metabolic disorders (B2.2/B3). The Down syndrome phenotype is more diagnostically relevant to Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) than metabolic causes."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Duodenal Atresia (Double Bubble Pattern in Early Neonates)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.019509877200497374, "posterior": 0.019509877200497374, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Timing of symptom onset (day 1-2 typical for duodenal atresia)", "History of abdominal girth asymmetry"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Abdominal ultrasound/flat plain radiograph for double bubble sign (air-filled stomach + proximal duodenum)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.55, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus (Whirlpool Sign on Contrast Study)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.019509877200497374, "posterior": 0.019509877200497374, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Subacute/episodic vomiting with sudden onset", "Presence of 'corkscrew' bowel loops on imaging"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Contrast imaging for whirlpool or malrotation confirmation", "Abdominal ultrasound for bowel wall edema/sentinel vessels"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Segmental Jejuno-Ileal Atresia (Stepladder Pattern on Contrast Study)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.01625823100041448, "posterior": 0.01625823100041448, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Microcolon appearance on radiograph vs Hirschsprung disease's 'lead pipe' colon", "Absence of congenital syndrome markers (e.g., Down syndrome)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Contrast enema for stepladder/transition zone vs volvulus's closed-loop vs atresia's air in the mid-small bowel"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Meconium Ileus (Cystic Fibrosis-Related Intrauterine Bowel Obstruction)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.006503292400165792, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B2.1", "B2.1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of abdominal distension from meconium plugs (meconium ileus classically shows proximal distended loop) argue against B2's motility hypothesis?", "result_summary": "Absence of meconium plug pattern in radiograph reduces likelihood of meconium ileus but doesn't directly contradict motility disorders like Hirschsprung disease.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the mother's refusal of prenatal testing and the lack of maternal diabetes history eliminate inborn errors of metabolism (B3) as a plausible explanation?", "result_summary": "Prenatal testing refusal doesn't exclude inborn errors like cystic fibrosis, though maternal characteristics provide limited direct supportive evidence.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "weak_for"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of Down syndrome physical findings (upslanting palpebral fissures, single transverse palmar crease) and rectal exam showing partial stool/flatulence evacuation provide specific evidence for Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) over an inborn error of intestinal epithelial function (B2.2)?", "result_summary": "Down syndrome physical features and rectal flatus evacuation on exam provide specific support for Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) relative to inborn errors (B2.2).", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B2.1": "moderate_for", "B2.2": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the clinical findings of unpassed stool since birth and neonatal onset of symptoms more strongly support inborn errors of metabolism than an acquired or syndromic condition?", "result_summary": "Neonatal onset of bowel dysfunction is highly consistent with congenital conditions like Hirschsprung disease (B2.1), while inborn errors (B2.2) are more likely to present with electrolyte/absorption abnormalities.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_for", "B2.1": "moderate_for", "B2.2": "moderate_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the unremarkable siblings’ health history and non-prenatal maternal diabetes status reduce the likelihood of inborn errors of metabolism in this case, given the strong Down syndrome phenotype favoring Hirschsprung disease?", "result_summary": "Healthy siblings reduce support for inherited metabolic disorders (B2.2/B3). The Down syndrome phenotype is more diagnostically relevant to Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) than metabolic causes.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2.1": "weak_for", "B2.2": "moderate_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "result_summary": "The presence of any stool/flatus passage (0.0-1.5mL) on rectal exam is relatively more consistent with Hirschsprung disease than with complete anatomic obstruction. No findings appear to contradict B2.1's leading position.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2.1": "neutral", "B2.2": "neutral", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.2186588921282799, "B2": 0.6647230320699709, "B3": 0.11661807580174928, "B2.1": 0.4653061224489796, "B2.2": 0.1994169096209913}, {"B1": 0.06178127780157502, "B2": 0.5296385383373423, "B3": 0.032950014827506674, "B2.1": 0.4732940129823059, "B2.2": 0.05634452535503642, "B1.1": 0.019509877200497374, "B1.2": 0.019509877200497374, "B1.3": 0.01625823100041448, "B1.4": 0.006503292400165792}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers present (airway not compromised, no severe respiratory distress, BP/pulse suggest hypotension but no overt shock signs, no signs of CNS herniation).", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "The highest posterior branch (B2.1: Hirschsprung disease) is 0.47, below 0.75, and the anatomic obstruction branch (B1) with 0.06 posterior remains a dangerous alternative (danger=0.8). Available discriminators like rectal biopsy are untried and could significantly clarify the diagnosis."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4732940129823059, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 2-day-old boy has an episode of vomiting in the hospital nursery. The vomitus was described as “bright green” without any traces of blood. The patient has urinated several times since he was born but has not passed any stool. He was born at 37 weeks of gestation to a 38-year-old G3P3 woman. The pregnancy was uncomplicated and the patient’s mother refused all prenatal testing. The patient’s 2 older siblings are both healthy. His temperature is 98.6°F (37°C), blood pressure is 67/43 mmHg, pulse is 135/min, and respirations are 34/min. On physical exam, the patient has upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, and a single transverse palmar crease. His abdomen is non-tender, firm, and distended. Bowel sounds are hypoactive. Digital rectal exam evacuates a small amount of stool and flatulence. A nasogastric tube is placed to decompress the stomach. The patient’s abdominal radiograph can be seen in Figure A.", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Duodenal atresia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Hirschsprung disease"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Intestinal malrotation"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Meconium ileus"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Pyloric stenosis"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Age: 2-day-old boy", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: vomiting in the hospital nursery with bright green vomitus and no blood", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Clinical history: urinated several times but no stool passed since birth", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Gestational age: 37 weeks (term birth)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Maternal history: 38-year-old G3P3 woman, uncomplicated pregnancy, refused all prenatal testing", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Siblings' health status: 2 older siblings are healthy", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "direct", "content": "Vital signs: T 98.6°F (37°C), BP 67/43 mmHg, pulse 135/min, respirations 34/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E8", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical findings: upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, single transverse palmar crease", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E9", "kind": "direct", "content": "Abdominal findings: non-tender, firm, and distended abdomen with hypoactive bowel sounds", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E10", "kind": "direct", "content": "Rectal exam findings: digital rectal exam evacuates small amount of stool and flatulence", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E11", "kind": "direct", "content": "Intervention: placed nasogastric tube for gastric decompression", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E12", "kind": "image_reference", "content": "Abdominal radiograph shown in Figure A — image not available in text form", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'aganglionic megacolon', 'Prenatal maternal abnormality', 'Maternal diabetes'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=71%, source=primekg]\n\nBowel Dysfunction Due to Inborn Errors of Metabolism vs Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, adducted thumb, failure to thrive in infancy, functional abnormality of the gastrointestinal tract, intestinal obstruction\n\nBowel Dysfunction Due to Inborn Errors of Metabolism vs Duodenal Atresia (Double Bubble Pattern in Early Neonates):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abnormal duodenum morphology, abnormality of the pancreas, abnormality of the pulmonary artery\n\nBowel Dysfunction Due to Inborn Errors of Metabolism vs Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus (Whirlpool Sign on Contrast Study):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification, abnormal joint morphology\n  Favours second: high forehead, intestinal malrotation, long palpebral fissure, neonatal intestinal obstruction, telecanthus\n\nBowel Dysfunction Due to Inborn Errors of Metabolism vs Meconium Ileus (Cystic Fibrosis-Related Intrauterine Bowel Obstruction):\n  Favours first:  abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification, abnormal joint morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal cortical gyration\n\nHirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon) vs Duodenal Atresia (Double Bubble Pattern in Early Neonates):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, adducted thumb, failure to thrive in infancy, functional abnormality of the gastrointestinal tract, intellectual disability\n  Favours second: abnormal duodenum morphology, abnormality of the pancreas, abnormality of the pulmonary artery, annular pancreas, autosomal recessive inheritance\n\nHirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon) vs Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus (Whirlpool Sign on Contrast Study):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, adducted thumb, failure to thrive in infancy, functional abnormality of the gastrointestinal tract, intellectual disability\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, autosomal dominant inheritance, frontal bossing, high forehead, intestinal malrotation\n\nHirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon) vs Meconium Ileus (Cystic Fibrosis-Related Intrauterine Bowel Obstruction):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, adducted thumb, functional abnormality of the gastrointestinal tract, intestinal obstruction, intestinal polyposis\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nDuodenal Atresia (Double Bubble Pattern in Early Neonates) vs Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus (Whirlpool Sign on Contrast Study):\n  Favours first:  abnormal duodenum morphology, abnormality of the pancreas, abnormality of the pulmonary artery, annular pancreas, autosomal recessive inheritance\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, autosomal dominant inheritance, frontal bossing, high forehead, intestinal malrotation\n\nDuodenal Atresia (Double Bubble Pattern in Early Neonates) vs Meconium Ileus (Cystic Fibrosis-Related Intrauterine Bowel Obstruction):\n  Favours first:  abnormal duodenum morphology, abnormality of the pancreas, abnormality of the pulmonary artery, annular pancreas, autosomal recessive inheritance\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nIntestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus (Whirlpool Sign on Contrast Study) vs Meconium Ileus (Cystic Fibrosis-Related Intrauterine Bowel Obstruction):\n  Favours first:  abdominal distention, autosomal dominant inheritance, intestinal malrotation, neonatal intestinal obstruction, telecanthus\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nRelated to Bowel Dysfunction Due to Inborn Errors of Metabolism: 4-hydroxyphenylacetic aciduria, 5-nucleotidase syndrome, abdominal obesity-metabolic syndrome\n\nRelated to Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon): hirschsprung disease, susceptibility to, intestinal motility disease, mendelian disease\n\nRelated to Duodenal Atresia (Double Bubble Pattern in Early Neonates): intestinal atresia (disease), non-syndromic gastroduodenal malformation, non-syndromic intestinal malformation\n\nRelated to Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus (Whirlpool Sign on Contrast Study): familial intestinal malrotation-facial anomalies syndrome, mendelian disease\n\nRelated to Meconium Ileus (Cystic Fibrosis-Related Intrauterine Bowel Obstruction): cystic fibrosis associated meconium ileus, ileus, intestinal obstruction in the newborn due to guanylate cyclase 2c deficiency\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 2-day-old boy has an episode of vomiting in the hospital nursery → nausea and vomiting → Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon)\n  A 2-day-old boy has an episode of vomiting in the hospital nursery → nausea and vomiting → Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon)"}
