diac Arrhythmias with Prolonged QT Interval vs Myocardial Injury or Ischemia:\n  Favours first:  bradycardia, palpitations, seizures, sudden cardiac death, syncope\n  Favours second: abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal facial shape, abnormal foot morphology\n\nRelated to Myocardial Injury or Ischemia: acute coronary syndrome, ischemic disease, myocardial disorder\n\n[RAG context for 'A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department after fainting' (score=0.63):]\n  Source: ECG Predictors of Cardiac Arrhythmias in Older Adults With Syncope. > Treatment / Management\n  Acquired QT Interval abnormalities: These are commonly iatrogenic, resulting from medications that prolong the QT interval. In such circumstances, offending drug discontinuation is sufficient therapy.\n[RAG context for 'A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department after fainting' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  With both CHS and EHS, heat-related reversible increases in cardiac biomarker levels are often present. Heatstroke often causes thermal cardiomyopathy. As a result, the CVP may be elevated despite sig\n[RAG context for 'He was outside tending to his lawn for several hours in the heat' (score=0.75):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  With both CHS and EHS, heat-related reversible increases in cardiac biomarker levels are often present. Heatstroke often causes thermal cardiomyopathy. As a result, the CVP may be elevated despite sig\n[RAG context for 'He was outside tending to his lawn for several hours in the heat' (score=0.82):]\n  Source: Heat exhaustion. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Specific heat illnesses can also have symptoms that mimic other syndromes. For example, heat edema should not be mistaken for swelling caused by other medical conditions, including hypervolemia from c\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  fainted after standing up suddenly in the heat → heat-induced orthostatic vasovagal syncope → Orthostatic Syncope in the Context of Heat Exposure\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess orthostatic vital signs and look for volume depletion signs given the hot environment context\n  recent over-the-counter ondansetron use → acute myocardial QT prolongation → Acute Ondansetron-Induced QT Prolongation without Ventricular Arrhythmia\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Measure QT interval on ECG and evaluate for electrolyte abnormalities (K+, Mg2+) despite lack of preceding palpitations\n  sudden orthostatic syncopal episode → hypovolemic state from dehydration → Hypovolemic Syncope from Volume Depletion\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Check for postural blood pressure changes and obtain skin turgor, mucous membranes assessment to evaluate hydration status\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
