{"case_id": "MB_9", "timestep": 3, "case_summary": "A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue. In general, he has been in good health; however, he recently has experienced weight loss, abdominal pain, and general fatigue. He has a medical history of anxiety, diabetes, a fracture of his foot sustained when he tripped, and a recent cold that caused him to miss work for 1 week. His current medications include metformin, insulin, buspirone, vitamin D, calcium, and sodium docusate. His temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 150/100 mmHg, pulse is 90/min, respirations are 18/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam reveals a calm gentleman. A mild systolic murmur is heard in the left upper sternal region. The rest of the physical exam is within normal limits. Laboratory values are ordered as seen below. Hemoglobin: 12 g/dL Hematocrit: 36% Leukocyte count: 66,500/mm^3 with normal differential Platelet count: 177,000/mm^3 Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase: elevated Serum: Na+: 139 mEq/L Cl-: 100 mEq/L K+: 4.3 mEq/L BUN: 20 mg/dL Glucose: 120 mg/dL Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL Ca2+: 10.9 mEq/L AST: 12 U/L ALT: 10 U/L Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma\nB. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia\nC. Chronic myeloid leukemia\nD. Leukemoid reaction\nE. Multiple myeloma\n", "root": {"label": "Subacute Constitutional Syndrome with Elevated White Blood Cell Count, Mild Systolic Murmur, and Possible Hematologic or Inflammatory Aetiology", "time_course": "subacute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.65, "supporting_facts": ["Recent fatigue, weight loss (7 weeks duration not explicitly stated but considered subacute by default)", "Leukocytosis with 66,500/mm³ absolute elevation", "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase", "Normal differential despite leukocytosis", "Mild systolic murmur in left parasternal region", "Borderline elevated temperature (37.5°C)", "Unexplained abdominal pain"], "excluded_candidates": ["Acute bacterial infection: Leukocytosis is present but normal differential and lack of infectious foci weakens this candidacy", "Diabetic ketoacidosis: Blood glucose is normal and there are no other metabolic acidosis markers", "Medication-induced leukocytosis (e.g., corticosteroids): No glucocorticoid use documented, but other medications remain in scope"], "alarm_features": ["Leukocytosis >6x upper limit of normal", "Constitutional symptoms (weight loss + fatigue)", "Elevated Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase in chronic myeloproliferative process context"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.050574380463839316, "posterior": 0.022283058508945054, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Subacute weight loss and fatigue (7 weeks) are more characteristic of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms. This provides moderate support for CML while reducing reactive leukocytosis plausibility.", "Elevated LAP has higher specificity for neoplastic (CML) than reactive leukocytosis. This moderately strengthens the chronic neoplasm hypothesis and weakens reactive etiologies."], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP is typical of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms (like CML) but lower in blast-driven myeloid neoplasms. This result weakly contradicts the 'increased blasts' subtype hypothesis.", "A preserved differential with leukocytosis is atypical for myeloid neoplasms with blasts. This weakly supports the idea of a chronic phase process (e.g., CML) and weakly excludes blast-driven myeloid neoplasms."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Presence of bone marrow fibrosis", "History of previous blood cancer", "Fever pattern or bleeding symptoms"], "requestable_discriminators": ["BCR-ABL1/FISH testing", "Bone marrow biopsy for blast count", "Cytogenetics panel"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.25, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5032873347301496, "posterior": 0.4434965302094608, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["A preserved differential with leukocytosis is atypical for myeloid neoplasms with blasts. This weakly supports the idea of a chronic phase process (e.g., CML) and weakly excludes blast-driven myeloid neoplasms.", "Elevated LAP has higher specificity for neoplastic (CML) than reactive leukocytosis. This moderately strengthens the chronic neoplasm hypothesis and weakens reactive etiologies."], "evidence_against": ["Splenomegaly is a key physical finding in CML. Its absence reduces suspicion for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms but doesn't rule it out definitively."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Recent weight loss vs chronic anemia history", "Splenomegaly on physical exam", "Family history of MPNs"], "requestable_discriminators": ["JAK2/V617F mutation testing", "CBC with peripheral smear review", "Abdominal ultrasound for splenomegaly"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.03819952887247724, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP has higher specificity for neoplastic (CML) than reactive leukocytosis. This moderately strengthens the chronic neoplasm hypothesis and weakens reactive etiologies."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.007958235181766093, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP has higher specificity for neoplastic (CML) than reactive leukocytosis. This moderately strengthens the chronic neoplasm hypothesis and weakens reactive etiologies."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.006684917552683516, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Subacute weight loss and elevated LAP are weakly suggestive of chronic myeloid neoplasms (e.g., CML chronic phase) and less likely for de novo AML due to its typically acute presentation and low LAP."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "CML Blast Crisis (Ph+ myeloid neoplasm with blast phase)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.008913223403578023, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of splenomegaly weakly contradicts CML blast crisis, as this phase often includes prominent organomegaly. However, it does not rule out other phases."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Therapy-Related AML", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.006684917552683516, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["A normal peripheral blood differential and absence of documented marrow abnormalities are moderate evidence against MDS/therapy-related AML, which typically presents with dysplastic features or blasts."]}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic Phase", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.34809320685044887, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of splenomegaly is not definitive against CML in chronic phase, but does moderately weaken it as splenomegaly is a typical finding. This remains within normal diagnostic uncertainty."]}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Polycythemia Vera (PV)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.06961864137008979, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Essential Thrombocythemia (ET)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.025784681988922142, "danger": 0.55, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the mild systolic murmur (possibly indicating increased blood viscosity) be explained by PV-related erythropenia?", "result_summary": "A mild systolic murmur could plausibly indicate PV-related polycythemia and increased blood viscosity, moderately supporting this diagnosis.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.2": "moderate_for"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal differential with leukocytosis and absence of blast morphologic features from the physical exam disfavor de novo AML (which typically presents with blasts) in this context?", "result_summary": "A normal differential with leukocytosis is neutral for de novo AML, though physical exam findings (no blasts) make this subtype less likely and more consistent with chronic processes.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of splenomegaly on physical exam contradict the expected findings in CML blast crisis, where organomegaly is typically prominent?", "result_summary": "Absence of splenomegaly weakly contradicts CML blast crisis, as this phase often includes prominent organomegaly. However, it does not rule out other phases.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.2": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 6, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal differential and lack of documented marrow cell morphology abnormalities argue against MDS/therapy-related AML, which typically have dysplastic or blasts in marrow?", "result_summary": "A normal peripheral blood differential and absence of documented marrow abnormalities are moderate evidence against MDS/therapy-related AML, which typically presents with dysplastic features or blasts.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.3": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 7, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would the absence of splenomegaly on physical exam significantly weaken CML chronic phase as a diagnosis, given that splenomegaly is a defining clinical feature?", "result_summary": "Absence of splenomegaly is not definitive against CML in chronic phase, but does moderately weaken it as splenomegaly is a typical finding. This remains within normal diagnostic uncertainty.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.1": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of normal peripheral blood differential and elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) rule out myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts as the cause of leukocytosis in this 59-year-old man, given that blast proliferation typically suppresses LAP synthesis?", "result_summary": "The elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) and normal peripheral blood differential are atypical for myeloid neoplasms with increased blasts. This supports chronic-phase myeloid neoplasms (e.g., CML) and excludes blast-driven processes like AML or CML blast crisis."}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.22672064777327935, "B2": 0.6558704453441296, "B3": 0.09716599190283401, "B4": 0.02024291497975709, "B1.1": 0.0680161943319838, "B1.2": 0.09068825910931175, "B1.3": 0.0680161943319838, "B2.1": 0.4919028340080972, "B2.2": 0.09838056680161944, "B2.3": 0.06558704453441297}, {"B1": 0.050574380463839316, "B2": 0.5032873347301496, "B3": 0.04334946896900513, "B4": 0.009031139368542737, "B1.1": 0.015172314139151793, "B1.2": 0.020229752185535726, "B1.3": 0.015172314139151793, "B2.1": 0.39502203598005925, "B2.2": 0.07900440719601186, "B2.3": 0.029260891554078468}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "Patient is hemodynamically stable with no acute life-threatening features requiring immediate action before diagnostic resolution.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis 'Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm' (B2) has posterior 0.503 (<0.75). No dangerous alternative (danger >=0.7) remains unresolved below posterior threshold."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.6, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.39502203598005925, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue. In general, he has been in good health; however, he recently has experienced weight loss, abdominal pain, and general fatigue. He has a medical history of anxiety, diabetes, a fracture of his foot sustained when he tripped, and a recent cold that caused him to miss work for 1 week. His current medications include metformin, insulin, buspirone, vitamin D, calcium, and sodium docusate. His temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 150/100 mmHg, pulse is 90/min, respirations are 18/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam reveals a calm gentleman. A mild systolic murmur is heard in the left upper sternal region. The rest of the physical exam is within normal limits. Laboratory values are ordered as seen below. Hemoglobin: 12 g/dL Hematocrit: 36% Leukocyte count: 66,500/mm^3 with normal differential Platelet count: 177,000/mm^3 Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase: elevated Serum: Na+: 139 mEq/L Cl-: 100 mEq/L K+: 4.3 mEq/L BUN: 20 mg/dL Glucose: 120 mg/dL Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL Ca2+: 10.9 mEq/L AST: 12 U/L ALT: 10 U/L", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: fatigue", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Symptoms: weight loss for several weeks", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Symptoms: abdominal pain", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Symptoms: general fatigue (distinct from chief complaint)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Medical history: anxiety disorder", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Medical history: diabetes mellitus", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metad