{"state": {"case_id": "MB_6", "timestep": 4, "case_summary": "A 2-day-old boy has an episode of vomiting in the hospital nursery. The vomitus was described as “bright green” without any traces of blood. The patient has urinated several times since he was born but has not passed any stool. He was born at 37 weeks of gestation to a 38-year-old G3P3 woman. The pregnancy was uncomplicated and the patient’s mother refused all prenatal testing. The patient’s 2 older siblings are both healthy. His temperature is 98.6°F (37°C), blood pressure is 67/43 mmHg, pulse is 135/min, and respirations are 34/min. On physical exam, the patient has upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, and a single transverse palmar crease. His abdomen is non-tender, firm, and distended. Bowel sounds are hypoactive. Digital rectal exam evacuates a small amount of stool and flatulence. A nasogastric tube is placed to decompress the stomach. The patient’s abdominal radiograph can be seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Duodenal atresia\nB. Hirschsprung disease\nC. Intestinal malrotation\nD. Meconium ileus\nE. Pyloric stenosis\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Neonatal Vomiting with Feacal Incontinence/Non-passing Stool and Distended Abdomen (Intestinal Obstruction Pattern), with Uncharacterised Abdominal Radiograph Abnormality", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.6, "supporting_facts": ["2-day-old age with acute presentation of bright green vomiting (suggesting bile reflux from distal obstruction)", "No stool passed since birth despite urinary output", "Distended abdomen with hypoactive bowel sounds", "Rectal exam yields small stool/flatulence (inconsistent with complete obstructive pattern)", "Tier-1 unresolved abdominal imaging available (Figure A)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Chronic Gastrointestinal Malabsorption Syndrome: inconsistent temporal pattern (acute presentation)", "Maternal Gestational Diabetes Sequelae: patient's mother's refusal of prenatal testing weakens this as primary mechanism compared to structural obstruction"], "alarm_features": ["Congenital anomaly markers (upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, single transverse palmar crease; consistent with Down syndrome)", "Hirschsprung's disease association with physical features and bowel dysfunction"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Anatomic Gastrointestinal Obstruction in Neonates", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.06178127780157502, "posterior": 0.07195541432000878, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Non-bilious green呕吐 in a term neonate with delayed stool passage is moderately suggestive of anatomic obstruction, though rectal evacuation weakens this pattern.", "Neonatal onset of bowel dysfunction is highly consistent with congenital conditions like Hirschsprung disease (B2.1), while inborn errors (B2.2) are more likely to present with electrolyte/absorption abnormalities."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of meconium plug pattern in radiograph reduces likelihood of meconium ileus but doesn't directly contradict motility disorders like Hirschsprung disease.", "Down syndrome physical features and rectal flatus evacuation on exam provide specific support for Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) relative to inborn errors (B2.2)."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Age at onset of symptoms (day 1-3 life typical for duodenal atresia/malrotation vs. meconium ileus)", "Presence of maternal familial history of congenital anomalies"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Abdominal ultrasound for duodenal obstruction (double bubble sign)", "Contrast enema to assess rectal transitional zone in Hirschsprung disease"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Neonatal Intestinal Motility Disorders", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5296385383373423, "posterior": 0.5171314990635759, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Classic physical stigmata of Down syndrome increase suspicion for Hirschsprung disease, which presents with delayed stool passage and rectal evacuation. This clinically supports B2 over B1.", "Rectal evacuation of stool and flatus suggests partial intestinal patency, which is more consistent with Hirschsprung disease than a complete anatomical block."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Physical exam signs of congenital syndromes (e.g., Down syndrome features)", "Bowel motility during clinical exam (delayed peristalsis or 'lead pipe' abdomen)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Rectal biopsy for megacolon detection in Hirschsprung disease", "Abdominal X-ray (Figure A) for Hirschsprung's 'tapered rectum' sign"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Bowel Dysfunction Due to Inborn Errors of Metabolism", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.021685212233701796, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Healthy siblings reduce support for inherited metabolic disorders (B2.2/B3). The Down syndrome phenotype is more diagnostically relevant to Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) than metabolic causes."]}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.4732940129823059, "posterior": 0.4800497861439459, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The presence of Down syndrome features and rectal evacuation in a term neonate with delayed stool passage strongly supports Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) due to its well-documented association with trisomy 21 and partial aganglionosis allowing gas/fluid communication.", "Down syndrome features and rectal flatus passage provide weak against evidence for malrotation/volvulus (B1.2), which presents more commonly with acute bilious vomiting and imaging findings of closed-loop obstruction rather than partial patency."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of delayed passage of meconium (in this case, no stool passed since birth supports the diagnosis)", "Presence of Down syndrome features (upslanting palpebral fissures, single palmar crease) which are strongly associated with Hirschsprung disease"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells confirming aganglionosis", "Rectal manometry (out-of-phase contractions) to differentiate from other motility disorders"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Neonatal Onset of Inborn Error of Intestinal Epithelial Function", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.03708171291963007, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Healthy siblings reduce support for inherited metabolic disorders (B2.2/B3). The Down syndrome phenotype is more diagnostically relevant to Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) than metabolic causes."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Duodenal Atresia (Double Bubble Pattern in Early Neonates)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.004293296670624926, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Passing of stool/flatus on rectal exam provides moderate against evidence for duodenal atresia (B1.1), as this diagnosis typically presents with complete gastric-duodenal obstruction and no distal bowel passage."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus (Whirlpool Sign on Contrast Study)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.008658497612806432, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Down syndrome features and rectal flatus passage provide weak against evidence for malrotation/volvulus (B1.2), which presents more commonly with acute bilious vomiting and imaging findings of closed-loop obstruction rather than partial patency."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Segmental Jejuno-Ileal Atresia (Stepladder Pattern on Contrast Study)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0013567942204861464, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Passing of stool/flatus through the rectum provides strong against evidence for jejunoileal atresia (B1.3), which typically presents with total intestinal obstruction precluding distal bowel communication."]}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Meconium Ileus (Cystic Fibrosis-Related Intrauterine Bowel Obstruction)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.006503292400165792, "posterior": 0.05764682581609128, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Maternal gestation age at delivery (37 weeks is term, but cystic fibrosis may have associated preterm labor in 30% of cases)", "History of respiratory distress or thick secretions (not mentioned in summary)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Abdominal X-ray for meconium plug/microcolon (not directly visible here but can be analyzed)", "Sweat chloride testing (strongly linked to cystic fibrosis)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}}, "frontier": ["B1.4", "B2.1", "B2.1", "B1.4"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would the rectal exam showing stool/flatulence evacuation (E10) definitively argue against jejunoileal atresia (B1.3) given its expected presentation of complete obstruction precluding distal patency?", "result_summary": "Passing of stool/flatus through the rectum provides strong against evidence for jejunoileal atresia (B1.3), which typically presents with total intestinal obstruction precluding distal bowel communication.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.3": "strong_against", "B2.1": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "result_summary": "The lack of any findings directly contradicting aganglionosis in an infant with Down syndrome features and partial distal patency actually neutralizes further against evidence for B2.1, as Hirschsprung disease's variable aganglionosis length can sometimes preserve minimal distal bowel passage.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.1": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the abdominal radiograph (Figure A) show any imaging features consistent with meconi