{"case_id": "MB_0", "timestep": 4, "case_summary": "A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department after fainting. He was outside tending to his lawn for several hours in the heat, when he stood up suddenly from pulling weeds and fainted. He denies any preceding symptoms and returned to baseline within 1 minute. The patient is not aware of any medical problems and just started seeing a primary care physician last week. He recently used a friend's prescription for ondansetron for nausea. His temperature is 99.3°F (37.4°C), blood pressure is 142/88 mmHg, pulse is 107/min, respirations are 14/min, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. Physical exam reveals intact cranial nerves, normal strength and sensation, and a stable gait. His abdomen is soft and nontender. An ECG is performed as seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis based on this patient’s ECG?\n\nOptions:\nA. Acute myocardial infarction\nB. Hypokalemia\nC. Intermittent torsades des pointes\nD. Previous myocardial ischemia\nE. Pulmonary embolism\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Syncope of Uncertain Aetiology with Possible Cardiac or Hyperthermic Aetiology and Uncharacterised ECG Abnormality", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.6, "supporting_facts": ["Sudden syncope during prolonged heat exposure and upright posture", "Recent ondansetron use (QT-prolonging medication)", "Tachycardia with no acute ECG evidence of torsades de pointes or ischemia", "Normal neurological and abdominal exam findings", "ECG image not available textually (Figure A reference) despite unexplained syncope"], "excluded_candidates": ["Vasovagal syncope: lacks typical prodrome but could still contribute in context", "Exercise-associated heat stroke: serum sodium unknown per POST-EXERTION rule", "Volume depletion: BP stable on presentation but duration of dehydration undetermined", "Neurological syncope: normal neurologic exams diminish this", "Myocardial injury: no acute E