/Autoimmune Pericarditis vs Apical Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy:\n  Favours first:  abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n  Favours second: abnormality of metabolism/homeostasis, ascites, asymmetric septal hypertrophy, autosomal dominant inheritance, autosomal recessive inheritance\n\nInfectious Pericarditis (Bacterial/Tuberculous) vs ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI):\n  Favours first:  cough, difficulty breathing when lying down, dry cough, dull chest pain, fever\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nInfectious Pericarditis (Bacterial/Tuberculous) vs Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI) or Unstable Angina:\n  Favours first:  cough, difficulty breathing when lying down, dry cough, dull chest pain, fever\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nInfectious Pericarditis (Bacterial/Tuberculous) vs Apical Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy:\n  Favours first:  anxiety, cough, difficulty breathing when lying down, dry cough, dull chest pain\n  Favours second: abnormality of metabolism/homeostasis, arrhythmia, ascites, asymmetric septal hypertrophy, autosomal dominant inheritance\n\nST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI) vs Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI) or Unstable Angina:\n  Favours first:  dizziness, fainting, heart palpitations, pain radiating to jaw, neck, back, arms, or abdomen, stomach pain or discomfort\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, diaphoresis, palpitations, syncope\n\nST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI) vs Apical Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abnormality of metabolism/homeostasis, arrhythmia, ascites, asymmetric septal hypertrophy, biventricular hypertrophy\n\nNon-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI) or Unstable Angina vs Apical Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n  Favours second: abnormality of metabolism/homeostasis, arrhythmia, ascites, asymmetric septal hypertrophy, biventricular hypertrophy\n\nRelated to Acute Viral/Autoimmune Pericarditis: infectious disease, pericarditis (disease)\n\nRelated to ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI): hemoglobinopathy\n\nRelated to Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI) or Unstable Angina: hemoglobinopathy\n\nRelated to Apical Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy: dilated cardiomyopathy, familial dilated cardiomyopathy, familial hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\n\n[RAG context for 'He states that the pain is dull in quality' (score=0.64):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Chest pain is usually present in acute infectious pericarditis and in many of the forms presumed to be related to hypersensitivity or autoimmunity. The pain of acute pericarditis is often severe, retr\n[RAG context for 'He states that the pain is dull in quality' (score=0.64):]"}
