{"case_id": "MB_6", "timestep": 4, "case_summary": "A 2-day-old boy has an episode of vomiting in the hospital nursery. The vomitus was described as “bright green” without any traces of blood. The patient has urinated several times since he was born but has not passed any stool. He was born at 37 weeks of gestation to a 38-year-old G3P3 woman. The pregnancy was uncomplicated and the patient’s mother refused all prenatal testing. The patient’s 2 older siblings are both healthy. His temperature is 98.6°F (37°C), blood pressure is 67/43 mmHg, pulse is 135/min, and respirations are 34/min. On physical exam, the patient has upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, and a single transverse palmar crease. His abdomen is non-tender, firm, and distended. Bowel sounds are hypoactive. Digital rectal exam evacuates a small amount of stool and flatulence. A nasogastric tube is placed to decompress the stomach. The patient’s abdominal radiograph can be seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Duodenal atresia\nB. Hirschsprung disease\nC. Intestinal malrotation\nD. Meconium ileus\nE. Pyloric stenosis\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Neonatal Vomiting with Feacal Incontinence/Non-passing Stool and Distended Abdomen (Intestinal Obstruction Pattern), with Uncharacterised Abdominal Radiograph Abnormality", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.6, "supporting_facts": ["2-day-old age with acute presentation of bright green vomiting (suggesting bile reflux from distal obstruction)", "No stool passed since birth despite urinary output", "Distended abdomen with hypoactive bowel sounds", "Rectal exam yields small stool/flatulence (inconsistent with complete obstructive pattern)", "Tier-1 unresolved abdominal imaging available (Figure A)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Chronic Gastrointestinal Malabsorption Syndrome: inconsistent temporal pattern (acute presentation)", "Maternal Gestational Diabetes Sequelae: patient's mother's refusal of prenatal testing weakens this as primary mechanism compared to structural obstruction"], "alarm_features": ["Congenital anomaly markers (upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, single transverse palmar crease; consistent with Down syndrome)", "Hirschsprung's disease association with physical features and bowel dysfunction"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Anatomic Gastrointestinal Obstruction in Neonates", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.07195541432000878, "posterior": 0.26009752060789404, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Non-bilious green呕吐 in a term neonate with delayed stool passage is moderately suggestive of anatomic obstruction, though rectal evacuation weakens this pattern.", "Neonatal onset of bowel dysfunction is highly consistent with congenital conditions like Hirschsprung disease (B2.1), while inborn errors (B2.2) are more likely to present with electrolyte/absorption abnormalities."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of meconium plug pattern in radiograph reduces likelihood of meconium ileus but doesn't directly contradict motility disorders like Hirschsprung disease.", "Down syndrome physical features and rectal flatus evacuation on exam provide specific support for Hirschsprung disease (B2.1) relative to inborn errors (B2.2)."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Age at onset of symptoms (day 1-3 life typical for duodenal atresia/malrotation vs. meconium ileus)", "Presence of maternal familial history of congenital anomalies"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Abdominal ultrasound for duodenal obstruction (double bubble sign)", "Contrast enema to assess rectal transitional zone in Hirschsprung disease"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role"