ool/flatulence evacuation (E10) definitively argue against jejunoileal atresia (B1.3) given its expected presentation of complete obstruction precluding distal patency?", "result_summary": "Passing of stool/flatus through the rectum provides strong against evidence for jejunoileal atresia (B1.3), which typically presents with total intestinal obstruction precluding distal bowel communication.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.3": "strong_against", "B2.1": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Hirschsprung Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "result_summary": "The lack of any findings directly contradicting aganglionosis in an infant with Down syndrome features and partial distal patency actually neutralizes further against evidence for B2.1, as Hirschsprung disease's variable aganglionosis length can sometimes preserve minimal distal bowel passage.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.1": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the abdominal radiograph (Figure A) show any imaging features consistent with meconium ileus, such as a meconium plug or microcolon, to strengthen its support for this cystic fibrosis-associated obstruction?", "result_summary": "The rectal exam findings of partial distal patency (stool and flatus) provide strong against evidence for jejunoileal atresia (B1.3), which typically presents with total intestinal obstruction incompatible with minimal distal bowel communication.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.3": "strong_against", "B2.1": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 1, "bun