ry in this otherwise healthy patient)", "Diverticular hemorrhage (uncommon in non-elderly individuals without constipation or prior diverticulitis)"], "alarm_features": ["Recent alcohol-induced gastropathy as potential precipitant", "Forceful vomiting causing transient systemic stress"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Hemorrhage from Hemorrhoidal Sources", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.4591693887074196, "posterior": 0.5822219018095306, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Alcohol-induced vomiting increases intra-abdominal pressure, heightening the probability of rectal bleeding from hemorrhoidal sources in this otherwise healthy patient.", "The lack of anal pain and physical findings (e.g., nodules on rectal exam) is typical for internal hemorrhoids, which usually present as painless bleeding, supporting (B1) over anal fissure/trauma scenarios."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Recent history of straining during bowel movements", "Presence of perianal pain/tenderness", "Family history of hemorrhoids"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Anoscopy to visualize internal hemorrhoids", "Colonoscopy to exclude other causes"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Colonic Vascular Malformation", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.025032241527086885, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Vascular malformations are more common in older populations. The patient's young age and lack of recurrence modestly lowers priority for (B2)."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Colonic Diverticular Hemorrhage", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.020025793221669506, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease with Colonic Injury", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.016020634577335602, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Colonic Malignancy with Ulceration or Complication", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0020025793221669507, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Grade I/II Internal Hemorrhoids (Painless Bleeding without Prolapse)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.4031731217918806, "posterior": 0.5605940451301276, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The lack of anal pain and perianal abnormalities on exam is a known clinical feature of non-prolapsed internal hemorrhoids (B1.1), providing weak but consistent support compared to other subtypes/algorithms with lower diagnostic confidence.", "The absence of localized perianal discomfort directly contradicts the expected presentation of acute thrombosed hemorrhoids (B1.2), which typically manifest with severe pain despite the presence of vomiting."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the hemorrhoid prolapse with straining and reduce spontaneously?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Anoscopy to confirm internal hemorrhoid grading", "Digital rectal exam for prolapse visualization"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "severity"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Thrombosed Internal Hemorrhoids with Acute Episode", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.021627856679403073, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of localized perianal discomfort directly contradicts the expected presentation of acute thrombosed hemorrhoids (B1.2), which typically manifest with severe pain despite the presence of vomiting."]}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Grade I/II Internal Hemorrhoids (Painless Bleeding without Prolapse)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "result_summary": "No definitive evidence contradicts Grade I/II internal hemorrhoids (B1.1) given their typical painless presentation. The absence of anal pain or prolapse on exam is expected for these subtypes.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "neutral", "B1.2": "strong_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral", "B5": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of alarm features (e.g., weight loss, anemia of chronic disease, family history of cancer) in this otherwise healthy 43-year-old with no constitutional symptoms rule out colonic malignancy as a cause of his acute hematochezia?", "result_summary": "The absence of alarm features (weight loss, family history of cancer, or chronic anemia) in a 43-year-old without constitutional symptoms significantly reduces the likelihood of colonic malignancy as a cause for his acute hematochezia, but does not completely rule it out. This result maintains the low probability assigned to malignancy in this cohort."}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of anal pain and lack of perianal abnormalities (e.g., nodules, prolapse) on rectal exam provide the best support for Grade I/II internal hemorrhoids (B1.1) over other subtypes or alternative diagnoses?", "result_summary": "The lack of anal pain and perianal abnormalities on exam is a known clinical feature of non-prolapsed internal hemorrhoids (B1.1), providing weak but consistent support compared to other subtypes/algorithms with lower diagnostic confidence.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "weak_for", "B1.2": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral", "B5": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's profuse vomiting last night with elevated intra-abdominal pressure provide a plausible explanation for acute thrombosis of an already dilated hemorrhoidal vessel (B1.2)?", "result_summary": "Vomiting-induced pressure could theoretically cause acute hemorrhoidal thrombosis (B1.2), though clinical evidence (absence of anal pain) suggests this remains unlikely in this case.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "neutral", "B1.2": "weak_for", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral", "B5": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Grade I/II Internal Hemorrhoids (Painless Bleeding without Prolapse)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "result_summary": "No evidence in the clinical presentation contradicts the typical painless profile of non-prolapsed Grade I/II internal hemorrhoids (B1.1), maintaining its diagnostic position.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "neutral", "B1.2": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral", "B5": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the complete absence of anal or perianal pain and normal vital signs directly contradict the clinical profile of acute thrombosed hemorrhoids, which typically manifest with localized or systemic symptoms?", "result_summary": "The absence of localized perianal discomfort directly contradicts the expected presentation of acute thrombosed hemorrhoids (B1.2), which typically manifest with severe pain despite the presence of vomiting.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "weak_for", "B1.2": "moderate_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral", "B5": "neutral"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.517561802349366, "B2": 0.03652621120916369, "B3": 0.02922096896733095, "B4": 0.02337677517386476, "B5": 0.014610484483665476, "B1.1": 0.4544445093799311, "B1.2": 0.06311729296943487}, {"B1": 0.4591693887074196, "B2": 0.032405247057603566, "B3": 0.02592419764608285, "B4": 0.020739358116866276, "B5": 0.002592419764608285, "B1.1": 0.4031731217918806, "B1.2": 0.055996266915538974}, {"B1": 0.5822219018095306, "B2": 0.025032241527086885, "B3": 0.020025793221669506, "B4": 0.016020634577335602, "B5": 0.0020025793221669507, "B1.1": 0.5605940451301276, "B1.2": 0.021627856679403073}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No acute life-threatening conditions or hemodynamic instability are present.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B1.1: Grade I/II internal hemorrhoids) has posterior 0.403 (<0.75). No dangerous unresolved alternatives with posterior >=0.10 remain (B5 malignancy at 0.0025). While hemorrhoid subtypes (B1.1 vs B1.2) may influence management choice (conservative vs surgical), actionable_parent_syndrome doesn't apply since B1's posterior is insufficient for management commitment. Available discriminators (anoscopy, DRE) could impact diagnostic precision."}, "turn_budget_used": 5, "estimated_remaining_value": 1.4, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.5605940451301276, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 43-year-old man presents to the emergency department with bright red blood in his stool this morning. He noticed this yesterday as well, but it was much scanter. The patient is otherwise healthy. He was celebrating his birthday last night and admits having \"too much to drink\" and vomited profusely overnight. He currently feels well and is not nauseous. He has no other significant medical history and does not take any medications. His temperature is 99.2°F (37.3°C), blood pressure is 110/75 mmHg, pulse is 82/min, respirations are 16/min, and oxygen saturation is 100% on room air. Physical exam reveals a nontender abdomen with normal bowel sounds. Rectal exam reveals grossly bloody stool. Laboratory studies are ordered as shown below.\n\nHemoglobin: 12 g/dL\nHematocrit: 36%\nLeukocyte count: 5,500/mm^3 with normal differential\nPlatelet count: 179,000/mm^3", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Erosion into a gastric vessel"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation and infection of colonic outpouching"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Submucosal esophageal tear"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular malformation"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: bright red blood in stool for 1 day (increased this morning)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Symptoms duration: 2 days (scanter yesterday, increased this morning)", "source_ids": [], "independent": tr