ina reduces likelihood of classic STEMI (B2.1) but makes atypical ACS variants (B2.2/B2.3) more plausible, particularly NSTEMI given the normal ECG described in the vignette.", "Positional chest pain variation is atypical for STEMI (B2.1), which is typically non-positional, but could be seen atypical ACS cases or in those with concurrent pericardial effusion."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the chest pain radiate to left arm/jaw?", "Was the syncope preceded by new-onset diaphoresis/nausea?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["ECG for regional ST elevation", "Serial troponin testing"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "management_pathway"}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI) or Unstable Angina", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.12115384615384614, "posterior": 0.10327633705972085, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The pattern of exertional worsening and syncope raises concern for hemodynamic instability in both HOCM (B3) and unstable angina (B2.2), but does not strongly differentiate between viral pericarditis (B1.1) and ACS due to overlapping features.", "Absence of prior angina reduces likelihood of classic STEMI (B2.1) but makes atypical ACS variants (B2.2/B2.3) more plausible, particularly NSTEMI given the normal ECG described in the vignette."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of ECG ST changes reduces support for NSTEMI (B2.2), but patients with chronic hypertension may exhibit minimal or atypical ST changes, maintaining low-to-moderate plausibility."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is pain relieved by rest/nitroglycerin?", "History of st